  "query_preview": "..\n\nKey Messages:\n\n* Reduced visibility and diminished air quality to continue across \n  the Northern Rockies\n\n* Thunderstorms capable of 50+ mph wind gusts across southwest \n  Montana and extreme northwest Montana, Wednesday evening\n\n* Gusty northwest wind Wednesday afternoon and evening to increase \n  wave activity on Flathead Lake\n\nFire Weather, Air Quality, and Wind Changes:\n\nA persistent dry airmass and daily late afternoon gusty winds will \ncontinue through Wednesday across the Northern Rockies. The \nafternoon winds may provide localized temporary relief from highly \nconcentrated wildfire smoke, however status quo in smoke conditions \nwill continue for the next few days. In fact, winds across northwest \nMontana are projected to shift to the north-northeast this evening \nthrough Thursday evening, however the airmass north and east of the \nregion has similar concentrations of local smoke. The most \nsignificant impact from the northeast winds will be increased wave \nactivity on area lakes.\n\nThunderstorms Beginning Wednesday:\n\nMoisture from west and south of the Northern Rockies will begin to \ncreep over the region on Wednesday afternoon and evening, with \nmainly dry thunderstorms capable of 50+ mph wind developing over \nsouthwest Montana and across extreme northwest Montana. Little \nappreciable rainfall is expected with the Wednesday storms, however \nmoisture will become much more available for subsequent days through \nthe weekend. In fact, a marginal risk of excessive rainfall arrives \nas soon as Thursday, with recent and ongoing wildfires having the \nhighest risk of runoff and flood impacts. The presence of wildfire \nsmoke will be a bit of wild card in the efficiency of \nthunderstorm development.\n\nBut does the wildfire smoke situation improve?\n\nThere will be some improvement in the wildfire smoke Wednesday \nthrough the weekend, however the improvement will be only \nincremental for most locations. The increased flow aloft does have \nthe potential to lift much of the existing smoke, however west and \nsouthwest winds aloft simply replace the smoke from upstream fires \nin Washington and Oregon. The best case scenario is for most smoke \nto be concentrated over terrain and only occasionally filtering down \nto the valley floors.\n\nBigger pattern change to come?\n\nLong range model ensembles suggest that a low pressure trough will \npersist off coastal western US next week. Most pattern solutions \nfeature either southwest flow aloft or a ridge of high pressure over \nthe Northern Rockies. Either way, the moisture from tropical storms \nin the Pacific Ocean will play a factor in whether temperatures \nreturn to summertime warmth or not. Wildfire smoke is still a big \nquestion mark next week.",
